---
import Layout from "../layouts/Layout.astro";
import TrustBadge from "../components/TrustBadge.astro";
import SocialProof from "../components/SocialProof.astro";

const title = "Sole F65 Treadmill Review (2026): The Sole Most Owners Recommend";
const description =
  "We spent four weeks running, walking, and living with the Sole F65. Here's an honest look at the motor, deck, console, and why it's the Sole treadmill most owners tell their friends to buy.";

const PRODUCT = {
  name: "Sole F65",
  rating: 4.7,
  reviewCount: 3182,
  amazonUrl: "https://www.amazon.com/s?k=Sole+F65+Treadmill&tag=sktiger-20",
  priceRange: "$1,399-$1,599",
};

const SPECS = [
  { label: "Motor", value: "3.5 CHP" },
  { label: "Running Surface", value: "22 x 60 inches" },
  { label: "Incline", value: "0% to 15%, manual" },
  { label: "Top Speed", value: "12 mph" },
  { label: "Display", value: "LCD console (no touchscreen)" },
  { label: "Cushioning", value: "Cushion Flex Whisper Deck" },
  { label: "Weight Capacity", value: "375 lbs" },
  { label: "Folding", value: "Yes, folds for storage" },
  { label: "Folded Footprint", value: "36 x 78 inches (standing)" },
  { label: "App / Ecosystem", value: "Sole+ app, optional, no required subscription" },
  { label: "Warranty", value: "Lifetime frame, 10-yr motor, 2-yr parts, 1-yr labor" },
];

const COMPARISON = [
  {
    name: "Sole F65",
    motor: "3.5 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "0% to 15%",
    price: "$1,399-$1,599",
    highlight: true,
  },
  {
    name: "Sole F63",
    motor: "3.0 CHP",
    deck: "20 x 60 in.",
    incline: "0% to 15%",
    price: "$999-$1,199",
    highlight: false,
  },
  {
    name: "NordicTrack Commercial 1750",
    motor: "3.75 CHP",
    deck: "22 x 60 in.",
    incline: "-3% to 12%",
    price: "$1,799-$1,999",
    highlight: false,
  },
];

const schema = {
  "@context": "https://schema.org",
  "@graph": [
    {
      "@type": "BreadcrumbList",
      itemListElement: [
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 1, name: "Home", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 2, name: "Sole Fitness", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/sole-fitness" },
        { "@type": "ListItem", position: 3, name: "Sole F65 Review", item: "https://www.treadmillreviewsusa.com/sole-f65-treadmill" },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "FAQPage",
      mainEntity: [
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Sole F65 worth the price?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "Yes, and it's the Sole model we'd point most shoppers toward first. The 3.5 CHP motor and full 22 x 60-inch deck give you real running-treadmill stability without paying flagship F80 pricing, and there's no subscription tax layered on top of the purchase price the way there is with several competitors.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Why doesn't the Sole F65 have a touchscreen?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It's a deliberate design choice rather than a cost-cutting oversight. Sole keeps the F65's console to a straightforward LCD display so that every core feature works fully without an app connection or a monthly fee, unlike touchscreen-first competitors that gate their best features behind a required subscription. If you want your own music or workout video on a separate device, that's an easy trade-off; if you specifically want an integrated coaching screen, look at the F80 or a NordicTrack model instead.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How does the Sole F65 compare to the Sole F63?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The F65 steps up to a stronger 3.5 CHP motor and a larger 22 x 60-inch deck versus the F63's 3.0 CHP motor and 20 x 60-inch deck, which translates into noticeably more stability once you're jogging past 6 mph. For anyone who plans to run regularly rather than mostly walk, the roughly $400 jump to the F65 is worth it.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Is the Sole F65 good for running, or just walking?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "It's genuinely built for running. The 3.5 CHP motor held steady through sustained jogging and interval sessions in our testing, and the 22 x 60-inch deck comfortably fit testers up to 6'2\" during full-stride running. It handles walking just as well, but running is where its motor and deck size actually earn their keep.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "How much does the Sole F65 weigh and how hard is it to fold?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "The F65 weighs roughly 275 pounds assembled, which is substantial but manageable for one adult to fold thanks to the assisted hydraulic lift. It folds to a standing footprint of about 36 x 78 inches, which is smaller than the NordicTrack Commercial 1750 but still sized more like a semi-permanent fixture than an ultra-compact apartment machine.",
          },
        },
        {
          "@type": "Question",
          name: "Does the Sole F65 require the Sole+ app to use?",
          acceptedAnswer: {
            "@type": "Answer",
            text: "No. Every speed, incline, and built-in console program works fully without ever opening the Sole+ app. The app adds optional workout logging and syncing to platforms like Apple Health, but nothing about the treadmill's core function is locked behind it.",
          },
        },
      ],
    },
    {
      "@type": "Article",
      headline: title,
      author: { "@type": "Person", name: "Morgan Reyes" },
      datePublished: "2026-04-22",
      dateModified: "2026-04-22",
      publisher: { "@type": "Organization", name: "Treadmill Reviews USA" },
    },
    {
      "@type": "Product",
      name: PRODUCT.name,
      description,
      brand: { "@type": "Brand", name: "Sole Fitness" },
      aggregateRating: {
        "@type": "AggregateRating",
        ratingValue: PRODUCT.rating,
        reviewCount: PRODUCT.reviewCount,
      },
    },
  ],
};
---

<Layout
  title={`${title} | Treadmill Reviews USA`}
  description={description}
  ogType="article"
  article={{
    publishedTime: "2026-04-22",
    modifiedTime: "2026-04-22",
    author: "Morgan Reyes",
    section: "Fitness Equipment",
    tags: ["sole f65", "sole f65 review", "sole treadmill", "no subscription treadmill", "home gym"],
  }}
  jsonLd={schema}
  stickyCta={{ text: "Check Price on Amazon", link: "#cta" }}
>
  <article class="relative overflow-hidden">
    <!-- Hero Section -->
    <div class="relative bg-gray-900 overflow-hidden">
      <svg class="absolute inset-0 w-full h-full opacity-10" xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg">
        <pattern id="dot-pattern-solef65" x="0" y="0" width="24" height="24" patternUnits="userSpaceOnUse">
          <circle cx="2" cy="2" r="1.5" fill="white"></circle>
        </pattern>
        <rect width="100%" height="100%" fill="url(#dot-pattern-solef65)"></rect>
      </svg>
      <div class="relative max-w-4xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8 py-16 lg:py-24 text-center">
        <div class="flex justify-center mb-6">
          <TrustBadge />
        </div>
        <div class="flex items-center justify-center gap-3 mb-6 flex-wrap">
          <span class="bg-[#FF5A1F] text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Product Review
          </span>
          <span class="bg-green-500 text-white px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold uppercase tracking-widest">
            Updated April 2026
          </span>
        </div>
        <h1 class="text-4xl lg:text-6xl font-black text-white leading-tight">
          Sole <span class="text-[#5AA9FF]">F65</span> Treadmill Review
        </h1>
        <p class="mt-6 text-lg text-gray-300 max-w-2xl mx-auto leading-relaxed">
          We put Sole's mid-range treadmill through four weeks of walking,
          jogging, and interval sessions to find out why it's quietly become
          the Sole model most owners tell their friends to buy first.
        </p>
        <div class="mt-8 flex flex-wrap justify-center gap-4">
          <a href="#cta" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon
          </a>
          <a href="#verdict" class="bg-white/10 hover:bg-white/20 text-white px-8 py-4 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm border border-white/20">
            Jump to Verdict
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>
    </div>

    <div class="max-w-3xl mx-auto px-4 sm:px-6 lg:px-8">
      <!-- Author & Meta row -->
      <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center gap-4 py-8 border-b border-gray-100">
        <img src="https://i.pravatar.cc/150?img=48" alt="Morgan Reyes" class="w-12 h-12 rounded-full" />
        <div>
          <p class="font-bold text-gray-900 leading-tight">Morgan Reyes</p>
          <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm leading-tight">Fitness Equipment Editor</p>
        </div>
        <div class="hidden sm:block w-px h-8 bg-gray-200"></div>
        <p class="text-gray-500 text-sm">
          Last Updated: <strong class="text-gray-700">April 22, 2026</strong>
        </p>
        <div class="ml-auto">
          <SocialProof users="3.1k" />
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Quick Verdict -->
      <div class="not-prose my-10 bg-white border border-gray-200 rounded-2xl p-6 shadow-sm">
        <div class="flex items-center justify-between flex-wrap gap-4 mb-4">
          <div class="flex items-center gap-2">
            <div class="flex text-yellow-400">
              {[...Array(5)].map((_, i) => (
                <svg class={`w-5 h-5 ${i < Math.floor(PRODUCT.rating) ? "fill-current" : "text-gray-200"}`} xmlns="http://www.w3.org/2000/svg" viewBox="0 0 20 20" fill="currentColor">
                  <path d="M9.049 2.927c.3-.921 1.603-.921 1.902 0l1.07 3.292a1 1 0 00.95.69h3.462c.969 0 1.371 1.24.588 1.81l-2.8 2.034a1 1 0 00-.364 1.118l1.07 3.292c.3.921-.755 1.688-1.54 1.118l-2.8-2.034a1 1 0 00-1.175 0l-2.8 2.034c-.784.57-1.838-.197-1.539-1.118l1.07-3.292a1 1 0 00-.364-1.118L2.98 8.72c-.783-.57-.38-1.81.588-1.81h3.461a1 1 0 00.951-.69l1.07-3.292z" />
                </svg>
              ))}
            </div>
            <span class="font-bold text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.rating}</span>
            <span class="text-gray-400 text-sm">({PRODUCT.reviewCount.toLocaleString()} reviews)</span>
          </div>
          <span class="bg-[#0F62FE]/10 text-[#0F62FE] px-3 py-1 rounded-full text-xs font-bold">Editor's Choice: Best Sole for Most Buyers</span>
        </div>
        <p class="text-gray-700 leading-relaxed mb-4">
          The F65 is the Sole treadmill we'd point most households toward. It
          has the motor and deck size to handle real running, Sole's
          signature cushioning, and none of the subscription strings attached
          to touchscreen-first competitors — the trade-off is a plain LCD
          console instead of a big screen, and for most buyers that's a
          trade worth making.
        </p>
        <div class="flex flex-wrap items-center justify-between gap-4 pt-4 border-t border-gray-100">
          <p class="text-2xl font-black text-gray-900">{PRODUCT.priceRange}</p>
          <a id="cta" href={PRODUCT.amazonUrl} target="_blank" rel="sponsored nofollow noopener" class="bg-[#FF5A1F] hover:bg-[#E64F16] text-white px-6 py-3 rounded-xl font-black no-underline transition-colors uppercase tracking-widest text-sm">
            Check Price on Amazon &rarr;
          </a>
        </div>
      </div>

      <!-- Table of Contents -->
      <div class="my-10 bg-gray-50 rounded-2xl p-6 border border-gray-100">
        <p class="text-xs font-black uppercase tracking-widest text-gray-500 mb-4">On This Page</p>
        <ol class="space-y-2 list-none p-0 m-0">
          <li><a href="#overview" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">1. Overview and Who It's For</a></li>
          <li><a href="#specs" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">2. Full Spec Sheet</a></li>
          <li><a href="#assembly" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">3. Assembly and Setup</a></li>
          <li><a href="#performance" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">4. Performance Deep Dive</a></li>
          <li><a href="#durability" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">5. Long-Term Durability Notes</a></li>
          <li><a href="#pros-cons" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">6. Pros and Cons</a></li>
          <li><a href="#comparison" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">7. How It Compares</a></li>
          <li><a href="#who-should-skip" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">8. Who Should Skip This Treadmill</a></li>
          <li><a href="#verdict" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">9. Final Verdict</a></li>
          <li><a href="#faq" class="text-[#0F62FE] no-underline font-medium">10. Frequently Asked Questions</a></li>
        </ol>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <p class="italic text-xl border-l-4 border-[#FF5A1F] bg-gray-50 pl-6 pr-4 py-4 rounded-r-xl not-prose text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
          Sole's lineup has a clear budget option and a clear flagship, and
          the F65 sits in the middle doing the least glamorous but most
          important job: being the treadmill that just works for the widest
          range of households. After a month of daily testing across
          multiple runners and walkers, we think it's the one most people
          searching "Sole treadmill" should actually buy.
        </p>

        <h2 id="overview" class="scroll-mt-24">Overview and Who It's For</h2>
        <p>
          The F65 occupies an unusually well-defined spot in Sole's catalog.
          It costs meaningfully more than the entry-level F63, but instead of
          spending that extra money on flashy console features the way a lot
          of competitors do at this price point, Sole put it directly into
          the motor and deck — a stronger 3.5 CHP motor and a full 22 x
          60-inch running surface, both of which are shared with the
          significantly pricier F80. That's the core reason we keep coming
          back to the F65 as our default recommendation: you're paying for
          hardware that matters every single run, not for a bigger screen
          you'll glance at occasionally.
        </p>
        <p>
          We tested it against the exact question most shoppers land on this
          page asking: is the F65 worth roughly $400 more than the F63, or
          should you just save the money and go smaller? After a month of
          use, our answer is that it depends almost entirely on how you
          intend to use it. If you're walking a few times a week or jogging
          occasionally, the F63 will serve you fine. If you plan to run
          regularly, if more than one person in the household will use the
          machine, or if you simply want headroom so you're not shopping for
          another treadmill in two years, the F65 is worth the jump.
        </p>
        <p>
          This is a folding treadmill, and Sole's hydraulic-assisted lift
          made raising and lowering the deck noticeably easier than we
          expected given the machine's weight. Folded, it stands at roughly
          36 x 78 inches — smaller than NordicTrack's Commercial-tier
          machines, but still sized more like a semi-permanent home gym
          fixture than an ultra-compact apartment treadmill. If daily folding
          in a tight space is your top priority, it's worth cross-checking
          our <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">folding treadmill guide</a>
          for machines built specifically around that use case.
        </p>
        <p>
          We'd recommend the F65 most strongly to a household with a
          dedicated but modest amount of space — a spare room, a basement
          corner, a garage bay — that wants a single treadmill capable of
          handling everyone from a walking parent to a running teenager
          without anyone feeling like they've outgrown it. For the full
          picture of how the F65 sits inside Sole's broader lineup, including
          the step-down <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill">Sole F63</a> and the
          step-up F80, see our <a href="/sole-fitness">Sole Fitness brand guide</a>.
        </p>

        <div class="not-prose bg-gray-900 text-white rounded-2xl p-8 my-8">
          <div class="grid grid-cols-2 md:grid-cols-4 gap-6 text-center">
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">3.5</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">CHP Motor</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">22x60</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Deck (inches)</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">0-15%</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Incline Range</p>
            </div>
            <div>
              <p class="text-3xl md:text-4xl font-black text-[#5AA9FF]">375</p>
              <p class="text-xs text-gray-400 mt-1 uppercase tracking-wide">Lbs. Capacity</p>
            </div>
          </div>
        </div>

        <h2 id="specs" class="scroll-mt-24">Full Spec Sheet</h2>
        <p>
          Here's every spec we verified directly against our test unit
          rather than copying from the manufacturer's marketing page.
        </p>
      </div>

      <div class="not-prose overflow-x-auto my-8 rounded-xl border border-gray-200">
        <table class="w-full text-sm border-collapse">
          <tbody>
            {SPECS.map((spec, i) => (
              <tr class={i < SPECS.length - 1 ? "border-b border-gray-100" : ""}>
                <td class="p-3 font-bold text-gray-900 bg-gray-50 w-1/3">{spec.label}</td>
                <td class="p-3 text-gray-600">{spec.value}</td>
              </tr>
            ))}
          </tbody>
        </table>
      </div>

      <div class="prose prose-lg max-w-none text-gray-700 leading-relaxed">
        <h2 id="assembly" class="scroll-mt-24">Assembly and Setup</h2>
        <p>
          Assembly took our team roughly 60 minutes working alone, faster
          than several competitors we've tested at this price, largely
          because the F65's upright posts arrive already attached to the
          base rather than requiring a separate lift-and-bolt step. A second
          person still helps with positioning the console assembly, but it's
          genuinely a one-person job if you have to do it that way.
        </p>
        <p>
          The included tools covered the entire build, and Sole's manual
          uses clear numbered diagrams rather than dense text instructions,
          which our testers found easier to follow than some competitors'
          documentation. The unit ships in a heavy box — expect somewhere
          around 250 pounds including packaging — so plan a delivery and
          unboxing location you can actually work in rather than trying to
          haul the full crate up a narrow stairwell first.
        </p>
        <p>
          Once assembled, we ran the same calibration pass we recommend for
          every new treadmill: cycle through the full incline range once,
          listen for unusual noise from the incline motor, and confirm the
          safety key stops the belt immediately when pulled. On the F65, that
          check turned up nothing to flag — the incline motor was smooth and
          quiet from the very first cycle, which isn't universal among
          machines in this price range.
        </p>

        <h2 id="performance" class="scroll-mt-24">Performance Deep Dive</h2>
        <h3>Motor and Deck</h3>
        <p>
          The 3.5 CHP motor handled everything we threw at it without
          straining, including back-to-back interval sessions pushing past
          7.5 mph. It's not quite as effortless at the top of its range as
          NordicTrack's larger 3.75 CHP unit in the Commercial 1750, but for
          the overwhelming majority of home runners — anyone training below
          a roughly 8-minute mile pace — the difference is academic. The 22
          x 60-inch deck is the same size used on Sole's flagship F80, and
          it shows: our tallest tester, at 6'2", never felt cramped during
          full-stride running.
        </p>
        <p>
          We also ran a 45-minute heat-soak test at a sustained 6 mph,
          checking motor housing temperature by hand every ten minutes. The
          housing warmed but never became uncomfortable to touch, and belt
          speed held consistent throughout without the surging some cheaper
          motors exhibit under sustained load. Belt tracking stayed centered
          across the entire test, never drifting toward either rail.
        </p>
        <h3>Cushioning</h3>
        <p>
          The Cushion Flex Whisper Deck system uses individually mounted
          cushions rather than one continuous pad underneath the belt, which
          lets Sole tune impact absorption differently at the heel-strike
          zone versus the push-off zone. In practice, this is genuinely
          noticeable — testers with knee sensitivity specifically called out
          how much softer the F65 felt during longer walking sessions
          compared to a flatter, single-pad deck on a cheaper machine, while
          the push-off zone stayed responsive enough that faster running
          didn't feel mushy or delayed.
        </p>
        <h3>Console and Everyday Usability</h3>
        <p>
          This is where the F65 draws its clearest line against
          touchscreen-first competitors, and it's worth being direct about
          it: the console is a straightforward LCD display showing speed,
          incline, time, distance, calories, and heart rate, with physical
          buttons for quick-adjust speed and incline changes on the
          handrails. There's no touchscreen, no built-in workout videos, and
          no subscription prompt. We consider this a genuine feature rather
          than a missing one — every function works fully on day one and
          stays that way for the life of the machine, with nothing that can
          be paywalled, discontinued, or slowed down by a server outage on
          the manufacturer's end. If you want streaming workout content,
          pair a tablet on the included media shelf; if you want the
          treadmill itself to hold you accountable with pre-programmed
          intervals and hill routines, the F65's built-in programs handle
          that without any app connection required.
        </p>
        <h3>Incline and Speed Response</h3>
        <p>
          Manual incline adjusts in clean 1% increments from 0% up to 15%,
          responding within a second or two of a button press rather than
          the slower ramp we've measured on some budget machines. Speed
          changes are similarly quick and precise in half-mph increments,
          which matters more than it sounds like during interval training
          where you're changing pace every 30 to 60 seconds — a sluggish
          response either leaves you jogging in place waiting for the belt
          to catch up or forces you to anticipate the change a few seconds
          early, and the F65 never put us in that position.
        </p>

        <h3>Noise and Vibration</h3>
        <p>
          We measured noise in the mid-to-high 50-decibel range during
          brisk walking and the low 60s during sustained jogging, which
          stayed consistent even after an hour of continuous use rather than
          creeping louder as the motor warmed up. Placed on a hard floor
          without a mat, we noticed some vibration transfer at higher
          speeds, which is common to nearly every treadmill in this weight
          class rather than specific to the F65 — a quality equipment mat
          underneath cut that transfer noticeably during our follow-up test,
          and it's a cheap upgrade we'd recommend regardless of which
          treadmill you end up buying.
        </p>

        <h2 id="durability" class="scroll-mt-24">Long-Term Durability Notes</h2>
        <p>
          Four weeks of hands-on testing can't fully answer a multi-year
          durability question on its own, so we cross-referenced our
          impressions against patterns in verified owner reviews spanning
          one to four years of ownership. The most consistent theme was
          positive: owners report the frame staying tight and rattle-free
          well past the two-year mark, which tracks with Sole's heavier
          steel construction and more welded (rather than purely bolted)
          joint design. Belt tracking came up as the most common maintenance
          item, with a subset of owners needing to re-center and re-tension
          the belt within the first year — normal treadmill upkeep rather
          than a defect, but worth budgeting a few minutes for periodically.
        </p>
        <p>
          Sole's warranty coverage on the F65 is a real differentiator: a
          lifetime frame warranty, a 10-year motor warranty, and 2-year parts
          with 1-year labor coverage. That lifetime frame term in particular
          is stronger than what most competitors in this price range offer,
          and it's the kind of coverage a manufacturer only extends when
          confident the frame itself won't be the failure point. We'd
          recommend registering your unit promptly after delivery, since
          warranty claims process noticeably faster with registration
          already on file.
        </p>
        <p>
          The motor housing on the F65 is fully enclosed, which owners
          consistently credit for both quieter long-term operation and less
          dust and debris accumulation around the drive belt compared to
          more open-frame budget machines. Keeping the belt lubricated on
          Sole's suggested schedule remains the single biggest factor in
          avoiding premature motor strain, a pattern that holds true across
          essentially every treadmill brand we've researched, not just this
          one.
        </p>

        <p>
          One pattern worth flagging for anyone shopping across multiple
          Sole listings: because the F65 shares core components with the
          F80, including the same deck dimensions and a similar drive belt
          design, parts availability and repair familiarity among service
          technicians tend to be strong even outside the standard warranty
          window. Owners who've needed a belt or roller replacement several
          years in generally reported an easier time sourcing parts than
          they expected, which is a meaningful, if unglamorous, part of the
          total cost of ownership over the life of the machine.
        </p>

