        <p>
          Stepping down to the <a href="/sole-f63-treadmill">Sole F63</a>
          saves roughly $400 but trades away two inches of deck width and a
          half-CHP of motor power — fine for walkers, noticeably tighter for
          regular runners. The NordicTrack Commercial 1750 costs several
          hundred dollars more and adds a large touchscreen with iFit
          coaching, but requires an ongoing subscription to unlock most of
          what makes that screen worth having in the first place. If you
          know you don't want a subscription tied to your treadmill, the F65
          delivers comparable core hardware for less money and no recurring
          bill.
        </p>
        <p>
          It's also worth a mental comparison to Sole's own flagship, the
          F80, even though it isn't in the table above. The F80 shares the
          F65's 22 x 60-inch deck but adds a stronger 4.0 CHP motor and a
          larger, more modern console with app integration — a genuine step
          up for serious daily runners, but overkill for the moderate,
          few-times-a-week use case the F65 is built around. Unless you know
          you'll be running most days of the week, the F65 gives you nearly
          all of the F80's day-to-day comfort for meaningfully less money.
        </p>

