        <h2 id="who-should-skip" class="scroll-mt-24">Who Should Skip This Treadmill</h2>
        <p>
          The F65 is our default recommendation in Sole's lineup, but it
          isn't the right machine for every shopper who lands on this page.
          If interactive, trainer-led coaching content with auto-adjusting
          incline is a must-have for you, the plain LCD console here will
          feel like a step backward — a NordicTrack or the Sole F80's more
          modern console will serve you better, even at a higher price. If
          you're a genuinely light user — walking occasionally rather than
          building a real routine — the F63 gets you Sole's build quality for
          $400 less, and you likely won't notice the smaller motor and deck.
          And if ultra-compact apartment storage is non-negotiable, its
          folded footprint, while smaller than NordicTrack's Commercial
          line, is still sized more like a permanent fixture; check our
          <a href="/best-folding-treadmill-reviews">folding treadmill guide</a>
          for machines built specifically around tight storage.
        </p>

        <h2 id="verdict" class="scroll-mt-24">Final Verdict</h2>
        <p>
          The Sole F65 earns its reputation as the treadmill most Sole owners
          actually recommend because it doesn't cut corners on the two things
          that matter most day to day: motor strength and deck comfort,
          delivered without a subscription tax. The plain console is a real
          trade-off, not a minor footnote, and if interactive coaching
          content is what you're after, you should shop elsewhere with eyes
          open. But judged purely as a piece of exercise equipment — motor,
          deck, frame, cushioning, warranty — this is a treadmill built to be
          used hard for years, not one built to look impressive in a product
          photo.
        </p>
        <p>
          If you're still weighing the F65 against a short list of finalists,
          our advice mirrors what we'd say about any treadmill in this price
          range: buy based on the motor and deck you'll be living with every
          single workout, not the screen you might glance at occasionally.
          Screens age; a properly built frame and a full-size deck don't.
        </p>
